A||||14||SCCA FIERO||How much RAM can my computer hold?||||1||
Z||000000||SCCA FIERO||12-04-2001||01:17 AM||||My comp is about 4 years old. AMD K6 333, PC100, came with 32 MB of RAM. I added 64 MB a little later. Now I see they have 128MB sticks. Can I use 2 128MB sticks or just 2 64MB sticks?<P>Do you need more info on my computer?<P>Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ferrari_cdn||12-04-2001||01:20 AM||||You can use two 128 MB sticks.  Size doesn't matter.  (I think).<BR>Even 256 MB sticks would be fine.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ICEFierosig.gif"> <BR>AIM: CanadianFiero<p>[This message has been edited by Ferrari_cdn (edited 12-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||84_duke||12-04-2001||01:27 AM||||you should be able to use two of pretty much any level ram, just make sure you are running at the right bus speed... ie pc100 or 133 or you might get memory conflicts||||reg||1
Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||12-04-2001||01:30 AM||||They didn't have the 256 MB in my bus speed (100), but they did have them for the 133. The 133's won't work in my computer, right?<P>So I can use 2 128MB sticks?<P>Also, these are K-Byte brand. Is that good or bad, or does it even matter?<P>BTW, this really pisses me off. When I bought the 64MB stick about 3 years ago, it cost me 100 bucks! Now the 64MB is like 13 bucks.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ferrari_cdn||12-04-2001||01:36 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><BR>BTW, this really pisses me off. When I bought the 64MB stick about 3 years ago, it cost me 100 bucks! Now the 64MB is like 13 bucks.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Computer prices are falling rapidly.  But be glad that you dont have to cough up $150 for a 128 MB stick.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||My7Fieros||12-04-2001||02:33 AM||||Your computer will hold up to 256. Most newer ones will hold up to 512. Mine is 2 1/2 years old (eMachines 400i)...came w/ 32 megs, Ive got 128 in it now, but like yours will only hold up to 256.<BR>256 is really more than enough, unless your using/making some sort of video applications.<BR>You should have 2 slots in your 'puter for memory chips. You can use either 1 256 chip or 2 128's. The prices of memory chips now are incredibly cheap.||||reg||1
Z||000006||SCCA FIERO||12-04-2001||04:33 AM||||Thanks guys.<P>This is the greatest place on earth.<P>Can I get a hug now?  <BR>:::chin quivering::: <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Look for my next adventure in O/T very soon (computer game problem)||||reg||1
Z||000007||JasonS||12-04-2001||10:16 AM||||You need to check with your manufacturer before you go out and buy a 128 or 256meg stick. They should be able to tell you the max size of ram each slot will hold.  You might have to go with 2 64's.<P>Jason ||||reg||1
Z||000008||84Bill||12-04-2001||10:29 AM||||Most MB's if not all in that range will take the 128 meg modules. 256 is a bit shadey but again most Pentium MB's will accept them||||reg||14
Z||000009||Sootah||12-04-2001||10:29 AM||||133 will work in your machine, it will merely run at the 100. Your machine should also be able to hold at least 512 MB of memory. My suggestion would be to pop out your old ram (assuming you only have 2 DIMM slots) and put in 2 256 MB dimms. I'm a computer technician and where I work the 256 is only $36. A 128 is $24. 12 more dollars for 2x the memory.<P>Kevin Souter<BR>-Computer Depot (Technician)<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__tig_sig2.gif">          <BR><sup><A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Black_Beauty_-_Front_Right.jpg" TARGET=_blank>The Black Beauty</A> (85 GT) - <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Busted-Ass_SE.jpg" TARGET=_blank>Bustedato</A> (86 SE, Parts car)<BR><I>Never pull your pants down in Cosco, the dang monkeys will getcha</I></sup> <BR><sup><B>AOL IM</B>: KSSouter<BR><B>MSN IM</B>: I_R_Sootah@hotmail.com<BR></sup>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Sootah||12-04-2001||10:32 AM||||Also, ONLY get Micron memory. Yes, there are some other good brands out there, but I can't pull their names off the top of my head. RAM does purdy much everything for your machine. You don't want some low quality POS memory in there.<P>Also, you ever sell your car? Still have a Fiero at all?<P><B>PEACE <I>-Sootah</B></I>||||reg||1
Z||000011||JSocha||12-04-2001||11:26 AM||||My computer holds alot of RAM.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.twinoaks4fun.com/smilies/ram.jpg"> <BR>2001 RAM 3500 1-Ton, fully loaded and then some!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by JSocha (edited 12-04-2001).]||||reg||12
